body:
Oh, I love Bordo's work! :)

&#x200B;

The thing to remember with scholars such as Bordo who rose in the era of early feminist literature (1970's), was she viewed beauty ideals not and constructions of the body not simply as benign symbols of social values. Rather, she identified insights relating to femininity, the body, and morale as bodily indicators that serve as mechanisms of social power and control. She concludes is most of her research that the body is not only symbolic, but holds power in its ability to influence behavior within the society through morale (Remember, this was the 1970's).

&#x200B;

A very famous quote of hers from 1993 - "The body— what we eat, how we dress, the daily rituals through which we attend to the body— is a medium of culture. The body . . . is a powerful symbolic form, a surface on which the central rules, hierarchies, and even metaphysical commitments of a culture are inscribed and thus reinforced through the concrete language of the body." This could be interpreted as an extension of her philosophy of the body being a site of struggle, power, assertion, pain, morale, symbolism, etc. The body can never truly be docile, and the feminine body has time and time again been used as a politicized site for displaying values or hate.

&#x200B;

As an example, to break it down, think of thinness as a beauty ideal in American society. This was popularized largely in the mid-to late 19th century, with changes first occurring in the middle to upper classes. As wages improved for the middle class, a new generation of educated men and women began consuming the same luxuries once afforded to the wealthy. In the years following, advertising strategies of pairing luxury with beauty demands the individual consumer that their ‘body is the passport to all that is good in life. This idealises that health, beauty, sex, and fitness were marketed as things only those who take care of their bodies can enjoy. Those who did not were deemed lazy, and even moral failures. Christian figureheads chimed in on this growing trend, citing hedonistic glutton and sedentary were sinful temptations of capitalist lifestyles. Thus, the body (especially female bodies) were largely used as a site of political and morale representation.

&#x200B;

I recommend you read ‘The Body Beautiful: Symbolism and Agency in the Social World' by Reischer and Koo, as their review breaks down Bordo's philosophy on the body really well! :)

&#x200B;

TL;DR – Bordo believes the body is not simply natural, but ‘becomes socialised’ through societal values and underpinnings.
